---
import { states, stateOptions } from "../../lib/states";

const stages = [
  ["Scrape", "Fetch", "Ask for each page, and write down which answered", "1, this one"],
  ["Store", "Archive", "Save each page untouched, before any code reads it", "2"],
  ["", "Prove", "Make each page show it is the school and season asked for", "3"],
  ["Parse", "Parse", "Turn the page's tables into typed records", "4"],
  ["Build, in part", "Resolve", "One identity per team, one record per match", "5 and 6"],
  ["", "Check", "Gates that can stop a bad record", "7"],
  ["Build, in part", "Build", "Publish the site, the database and the journals", "8 and 9"],
  ["Report", "The coverage record", "Kept from Fetch onward, beside every step", "1, this one"],
];

const learnPrompt = `I'm learning how to tell whether a data collection is complete. Teach me with one running example that is not about sports.

1. What an expected set is, and why it has to be written before the collection runs and from outside it.
2. Coverage as what arrived divided by what was expected, and why the unit I count in (sources, records, rows) changes the answer.
3. The difference between a source that is empty, one I could not reach, and one I have no way to read, and what each lets me claim.
4. Missing completely at random, missing at random, and missing not at random, with one example of each.
5. End with three questions that check my understanding. Wait for my answers before you give yours.`;

const q = [
  { key: "timeout", text: "Pinecrest's results page timed out on every try.", answer: "unavailable", why: "Unavailable. It was asked for and did not arrive, and the reason is known." },
  { key: "header", text: "Oakfield's forfeits page arrived, shows this season, and its table has a header and no rows.", answer: "empty", why: "Empty. Served and proved, and it holds nothing. Here zero forfeits is true." },
  { key: "scans", text: "The league posts its judges' score sheets as scanned images, and the collector has no way to read them.", answer: "no-collector", why: "No collector. Retrying changes nothing until someone builds a reader." },
];
const captions = [
  ["found", "22 debates"],
  ["scheduled", "22 of 30 debates"],
  ["named", "22 of 27 finished debates. Pinecrest's page did not answer."],
  ["whole", "27 of 27 finished debates. Pinecrest's five were never held."],
];
---

<section id="coverage">
  <h2>The idea: coverage</h2>
  <p>Each of the three cases uses one idea. Here it is first, so you can watch for it.</p>

  <div class="cv-define">
    <p><dfn>Completeness</dfn> is the question: is everything here? <dfn>Coverage</dfn> is how you answer it. For each item you expected, compare what you expected with what arrived.</p>
    <p class="cv-formula"><span>coverage</span> = <span>what arrived</span> ÷ <span>what was expected</span>, counted in one unit</p>
  </div>
  <p>Three things make that ratio honest.</p>
  <ul class="cv-three">
    <li><b>The expected set comes first, and from outside.</b><span>Write the list of what should exist before the run, from something the run cannot change: a membership list, a competition's format. Counted from what arrived, a source that stayed silent drops out of its own denominator.</span></li>
    <li><b>The unit is the claim's unit.</b><span>Count pages and one silent site is one gap. Count matches and the same site may be several. Use the unit your claim is made in.</span></li>
    <li><b>Every expected item ends in a state.</b><span>A collector has two outputs: the data, and a <strong>coverage record</strong> of everything it expected and what happened to each item.</span></li>
  </ul>

  <p>These are the four states an item that was asked for can end in.</p>
  <div class="cv-states">
    {states.map((s) => (
      <div class={`cv-state is-${s.key}`}>
        <h4><i aria-hidden="true"></i>{s.name}</h4>
        <p>{s.means}</p>
        <p><b>Claim:</b> {s.claim}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <p>Unavailable and no collector are the two kinds of <strong>unknown</strong>: the first may close on a retry, the second only when someone builds a reader. An item nobody asked for has no state at all. It was <strong>never asked</strong>, and no record mentions it. Unknown is never zero.</p>

  <h4 class="cv-try">Where this sits in the pipeline</h4>
  <p>The film named Kick's five stages. The platform tour has seven steps, and the course takes one or two per module. Kick's word Build covers two of them.</p>
  <div class="cv-scroll">
    <table class="cv-stagemap">
      <caption>Kick's stages against the platform's steps</caption>
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Kick</th><th scope="col">Platform</th><th scope="col">What it does</th><th scope="col">Module</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {stages.map(([kick, step, what, mod]) => (
          <tr class:list={{ "is-skipped": !kick, "is-here": mod.startsWith("1,") }}><td>{kick || <span class="cv-none">skipped</span>}</td><th scope="row">{step}</th><td>{what}</td><td>{mod}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>Kick's Build picked which copy of each match to keep and then made the table. The platform's Build publishes finished products from checked records. Same word, two different steps.</p>

  <div class="cv-prompt cv-learn">
    <p class="cv-prompt-head">Learn more with AI</p>
    <p class="cv-prompt-note">Paste this into any AI assistant for a short tutorial on a different example. Then come back and check yourself on a season it has never seen.</p>
    <p data-copy-text tabindex="0">{learnPrompt}</p>
    <button type="button" data-copy>Copy the prompt</button>
  </div>

  <h4 class="cv-try">Check yourself: a season nobody has seen</h4>
  <p>A school debate league has six schools, and each hosts every other school once. Three debates are still to be held. A collector asks each school's results page, and reads each debate from its host's page. Five pages arrive and list 22 finished debates. Pinecrest's site times out, and Pinecrest has hosted five finished debates.</p>
  <div class="cv-check" data-once="idea">
    <div data-q data-type="number" data-answer="30" data-why="30. Six hosts, each with five visitors: 6 × 5, from the league's own rules.">
      <label for="cv-idea-total">How many debates should the season hold?</label>
      <input id="cv-idea-total" inputmode="numeric" autocomplete="off" data-save="idea-total" />
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    {q.map((item) => (
      <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer={item.answer} data-why={item.why}>
        <label><span>{item.text} What state is it in?</span>
          <select data-save={`idea-${item.key}`}><option value="">Choose a state</option>{stateOptions.map(([k, n]) => <option value={k}>{n}</option>)}</select>
        </label>
        <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
      </div>
    ))}
    <div class="cv-line" data-q data-type="select" data-answer="named" data-why="22 of 27 finished, with the silent page named. Not 30, because three debates cannot be missing yet. Not 27 of 27: Pinecrest's debates happened, and nobody has read them." data-wrong={JSON.stringify({ found: "A count with no denominator says nothing about what is missing.", scheduled: "Three of the 30 have not happened, so they cannot be missing.", whole: "Pinecrest's five were held. Its page is unavailable, which is unknown, not zero." })}>
      <label><span>Which caption belongs under the league table?</span>
        <select data-save="idea-caption"><option value="">Choose</option>{captions.map(([k, t]) => <option value={k}>{t}</option>)}</select>
      </label>
      <p class="cv-fb" role="status"></p>
    </div>
    <p class="cv-once">One attempt at all five.</p>
    <button type="button" data-submit>Check my five</button>
    <p class="cv-fb" role="status" data-need></p>
    <div class="cv-reveal"><p>That is the whole idea: an expected set from outside the run, a count in the claim's unit, and a state for every item. The three cases show what goes wrong when each is missing.</p></div>
  </div>
  <p class="cv-next">Case 1 is the expected set Kick never wrote.</p>
</section>
